WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.460
Since infants are so much smaller than adults

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:05.960
or even older children, our chest compressions technique

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.520
has to adapt to their size while still being powerful enough

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.640
to move blood to the brain.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:15.460
There are two primary ways to perform infant compressions.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:16.760
The one-handed technique

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.200
and the two-thumb encircling method.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.000
Depending on the situation and your own comfort,

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.600
you can choose the one that works best for you.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.299
Your instructor will demonstrate each technique,

00:00:27.299 --> 00:00:29.360
then give you a chance to practice.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:31.219
Before beginning compressions,

00:00:31.219 --> 00:00:34.459
ensure the infant is on a hard, flat surface,

00:00:34.459 --> 00:00:37.459
such as a sturdy table or even the ground.

00:00:37.459 --> 00:00:41.080
If they are on a soft crib mattress or a changing pad,

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.840
your compressions will just push them into the padding

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.680
instead of squeezing the heart.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.040
You need that solid resistance underneath them

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.520
to make every push count.

